          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a urinary tract infection, and why are they more common in women?",
          "answer": "A urinary tract infection (UTI) is a bacterial infection of the urinary system. Most involve the bladder and urethra, the tube that carries urine out of the body. This is called a lower tract infection, or cystitis. *Escherichia coli* causes most of them. If the infection reaches the kidney, it is called pyelonephritis, which is a more serious illness. (Source: General clinical reference, \"Urinary tract infection\")\n\n**Why women are affected more often:** The clinical reference lists female anatomy, with a shorter urethra, as a contributing factor. A shorter urethra gives bacteria a shorter distance to travel to reach the bladder. My search did not return any passage explaining the link between the urethra and the anus, so I cannot confirm that point from this source. Other contributing factors listed include sexual activity, some contraceptives (including spermicides and diaphragms), changes in vaginal bacteria after menopause, incomplete bladder emptying, catheters, kidney stones, diabetes, and previous infections. (Source: General clinical reference, \"Urinary tract infection\")\n\n**When to seek urgent care:** If someone with a suspected UTI has fever, feels very unwell or confused, or has other signs of a serious response to infection, they should seek care promptly. People over 65, pregnant people, and those with diabetes, weakened immunity, or a catheter are at higher risk. (Source: Sepsis guidance, \"Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection\")\n\nA clinician can help work out whether a UTI is likely and what treatment, if any, fits the situation. This is general information, not a diagnosis.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
